JOIN(1)                        P32W Unix                         JOIN(1)

NAME
       join - join the lines of two files on a common field

SYNOPSIS
       join [-a 1|2] [-v 1|2] [-e s] [-o list] [-t c] [-1 f] [-2 f]
            file1 file2

DESCRIPTION
       join reads two files sorted on their join fields (as by sort,
       comparing bytes) and, for each pair of lines (one from each
       file) whose join fields are the same, writes one line: the
       join field, then the other fields of the line of file1, then
       those of the line of file2. The join field is field 1 unless
       -1, -2 or -j say otherwise. A file named - is the standard
       input.

       By default fields are separated by blanks (spaces and tabs;
       blanks at the start of a line are ignored) and written
       separated by one space. With -t c every c separates two
       fields (so fields may be empty) and c separates the fields
       written. Lines may be of any length.

OPTIONS
       -a n   also write the lines of file n (1 or 2) that pair with
              no line of the other file; may be given twice
       -v n   write only the lines of file n that pair with none
       -e s   write s in place of an empty or missing field
       -o list
              write the fields in list instead: items N.M (field M
              of file N) and 0 (the join field), separated by
              commas or blanks
       -t c   fields are separated by the character c
       -1 f   the join field of file1 is field f
       -2 f   the join field of file2 is field f
       -j f   the join field of both files is field f

EXAMPLES
       join names ages
       join -t: -1 3 -2 1 -o 1.1,2.2 /etc/passwd groups
       join -v1 all done             lines of all not in done

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened.

SEE ALSO
       sort(1), comm(1), paste(1), cut(1)

BUGS
       join does not check that the files are sorted; if they are
       not, pairs are missed. The lines sharing one join field are
       held in memory. -j1 f and -j2 f (old forms) are not
       understood.
